{
  "dom_id": "step-q324152",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226602/assets/59ad56e7eae8e8598ebd511a7b499f35cd6a178405f8198831b23e8d5d47a513.png)\n\nThe circles $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $  intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
